From: SPNMX57 port
Subject: [PATCH] net: mdio: ipq4019: QCA8084 preinit v2, clock/reset/calibrate

Extends the read-only first cut with the full sequence reverse engineered
from the vendor U-Boot APPSBL partition via Ghidra decompilation of
FUN_4a94c630 (clock enable, reset pulse, per-port calibration) and its two
small helpers. See docs/uboot-qca8084-protocol.md and
docs/preinit-remaining-scope.md in louij2/linksys-spnmx57-openwrt.

Confirmed on real hardware first: the read-only v1 read EPHY_CFG as a
stable, sensible 0x00318820 (strap addresses 0,1,2,3) across two separate
boots, so the underlying 32-bit indirect register protocol is trusted
before this patch writes anything with it.

Sequence: enable+reset-pulse two shared clock domains, clear bit 0 across
eight registers in a 0x20-byte-stepped range (meaning not confirmed against
a datasheet, transcribed as-is), enable+reset-pulse the four per-EPHY clock
domains, then -- gated on a status register read matching the same gate
condition the vendor code uses -- calibrate each of the four EPHYs by
writing two extracted bitfields into its own MDIO debug registers 0x1D/0x1E
(the same convention mainline at803x.c already uses for other Qualcomm
PHYs), finishing with the EPHY_CFG bit[21:20] clear the vendor code also
performs at the end.

Every multi-bit constant not independently verified against a datasheet is
transcribed exactly from the decompiled bytecode rather than re-derived by
hand, and is commented as such.
---
--- a/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-ipq4019.c
+++ b/drivers/net/mdio/mdio-ipq4019.c
@@ -8,10 +8,13 @@
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_mdio.h>
 #include <linux/phy.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/mii.h>
 
 #define MDIO_MODE_REG				0x40
 #define   MDIO_MODE_MDC_MODE			BIT(12)
@@ -48,19 +51,1086 @@
 
 #define IPQ_PHY_SET_DELAY_US	100000
 
+/*
+ * QCA8084/QCA8386 preinit, reached only when the mdio node carries
+ * qcom,qca8084-preinit in the DTS. Reverse engineered from the vendor
+ * U-Boot's APPSBL partition (Ghidra decompilation, not a datasheet) --
+ * see docs/uboot-qca8084-protocol.md in the linksys-spnmx57-openwrt repo.
+ *
+ * The QCA8386 switch exposes its internal 32-bit register space over plain
+ * Clause 22 MDIO, indirectly, via a fixed pseudo-PHY pair:
+ *   - write the page (bits [23:8] of the logical address) to phy 0x18,
+ *     reg 0x0c, then wait ~100us for it to settle
+ *   - the 32-bit value then lives at phy 0x10, two consecutive 16-bit
+ *     registers (logical_addr & 0x1c) and +2, low half then high half
+ *
+ * This first cut is deliberately READ-ONLY. It reads EPHY_CFG (the register
+ * holding each EPHY's current strap address as a 5-bit field) and logs it,
+ * to settle whether the addresses already default to 1,2,3,4 in hardware
+ * before anything writes to a switch-internal register at all. The vendor's
+ * own U-Boot never programs EPHY_CFG either, only reads it, which is the
+ * whole reason this question is open.
+ */
+#define QCA8084_PAGE_PHY	0x18
+#define QCA8084_PAGE_REG	0x0c
+/* The data window is EIGHT consecutive pseudo-PHY addresses, 0x10 to 0x17,
+ * not one. The logical 32-bit address splits three ways:
+ *
+ *   bits [23:8]  the page, written to phy 0x18 reg 0x0c
+ *   bits  [7:5]  select WHICH data pseudo-PHY, 0x10 | (addr >> 5) & 7
+ *   bits  [4:2]  the 16-bit register pair within that pseudo-PHY, addr & 0x1c
+ *
+ * This driver used a constant 0x10 and therefore silently discarded bits
+ * [7:5], so every access aliased to a 32-byte window at the bottom of its
+ * page. Two independent observations on hardware forced this out:
+ *
+ *   - Dumping the fuse block 0x0c900000..0x0c900070 gave eight distinct
+ *     words repeating identically every 0x20 bytes. A 0x20 alias period is
+ *     exactly three dropped address bits.
+ *   - The write-mask probe reported MEM_CTRL at 0x0c90f044 as writable only
+ *     in bits [31:12]. 0x44 truncated to 0x04 is GLOBAL_INTR_STATUS, whose
+ *     low bits are genuinely read-only. It was never MEM_CTRL that refused
+ *     the write; the write never reached MEM_CTRL.
+ *
+ * The damage was wide. Anything at an offset of 0x20 or more inside its page
+ * went somewhere else entirely: WORK_MODE 0x0c90f030 landed on
+ * WOL_INTR_ENABLE 0x0c90f010, so the work mode was never set; the six clock
+ * branches 0x0c8001a8..0x0c8001bc landed on 0x0c800108..0x0c80011c; and the
+ * eight isolate registers 0x0c800058..0x0c800158 collapsed onto just two
+ * distinct addresses, which is precisely why their read-backs came out as
+ * six of one value and two of another.
+ *
+ * Registers that happened to sit below offset 0x20 -- EPHY_CFG 0x0c90f018,
+ * SERDES_CFG 0x0c90f014, the MDC resets at 0x0c800304 -- were correct by
+ * luck, which is why parts of the sequence demonstrably worked and made the
+ * addressing look sound.
+ */
+#define QCA8084_DATA_PHY(addr)	(0x10 | (((addr) >> 5) & 0x7))
+#define QCA8084_EPHY_CFG	0x0c90f018u
+/* companion register holding the next three MDIO addresses */
+#define QCA8084_MDIO_ADDR_CFG	0x0c90f014u
+/* cleared to 0 in bits [4:0] partway through the bring-up sequence */
+#define QCA8084_EPHY_HOLD	0x0c800304u
+/* read to decide whether the vendor runs the calibration loop */
+#define QCA8084_CALIB_GATE	0x0c900014u
+/* From qca-ssdk include/hsl/mht/mht_reg.h -- the vendor's own Linux driver,
+ * which ships in this build tree and is a far better source than decompiling
+ * the bootloader. WORK_MODE selects the package topology; MHT_SWITCH_MODE is
+ * BIT(WORK_MODE_PHY3_SEL1_BOFFSET) with the mode field being bits [5:0].
+ */
+#define QCA8084_WORK_MODE	0x0c90f030u
+#define QCA8084_SWITCH_MODE	0x10u
+#define QCA8084_MEM_CTRL	0x0c90f044u
+#define QCA8084_MEM_ACC_0	0x0c90f048u
+#define QCA8084_MDIO_CTRL0	0x0c90f03cu
+#define QCA8084_MDIO_CTRL1	0x0c90f040u
+/* fuse row carrying the SKU, per qca_mht_sku_check() */
+#define QCA8084_QFPROM_PTE_ROW0	0x0c900000u
+#define QCA8084_QFPROM_PTE_ROW2	0x0c900014u
+/* clock controller, per MHT_CLK_BASE_REG and the RCGR/CBCR bit definitions in
+ * qca-ssdk include/init/ssdk_mht_clk.h
+ */
+#define QCA8084_CLK_BASE	0x0c800000u
+#define QCA8084_SWITCH_CORE_CBCR	0x0c800008u
+/* bit 0 = GCC_GLOBAL_CTL_GCC_TOP_FUNC_ARES, aka MHT_GLOBAL_RST */
+#define QCA8084_GLOBAL_CTL	0x0c800308u
+#define QCA8084_RCGR_CMD_UPDATE	0x1u
+#define QCA8084_RCGR_SRC_SEL	0x700u
+/* RCGR_HDIV, bits [4:0]. The vendor's rate maths is prate/rate = (div + 1)/2,
+ * so div = (prate * 2 / rate) - 1.
+ */
+#define QCA8084_RCGR_HDIV	0x1fu
+/* register type tag qca-ssdk puts in the top nibble, zero for the QCA8386 */
+#define SSDK_SWITCH_REG_TYPE_MASK	0xf0000000u
+
+/* The bring-up sequence logs a lot: a read-back verdict for every register it
+ * writes, the strap addresses, the gate value, and a full 32-address bus scan.
+ * That is the right default while the port is being debugged and the wrong one
+ * on a device somebody is actually using, so it is gated on a DTS property.
+ * Set "qcom,qca8084-preinit-debug" alongside "qcom,qca8084-preinit" to get it
+ * back. Warnings are never gated.
+ */
+static bool qca8084_verbose;
+
+#define qca8084_dbg(dev, fmt, ...)					\
+	do {								\
+		if (qca8084_verbose)					\
+			dev_info(dev, fmt, ##__VA_ARGS__);		\
+	} while (0)
+
+static int qca8084_reg_read32(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 addr, u32 *val)
+{
+	int lo, hi, ret;
+
+	/* bus->read/write directly, NOT mdiobus_read/write: this now runs
+	 * BEFORE of_mdiobus_register(), and bus->mdio_lock is only
+	 * initialised INSIDE __mdiobus_register() (mutex_init(&bus->mdio_lock)
+	 * happens there, not at allocation -- checked against the actual
+	 * kernel source after an earlier version of this patch silently read
+	 * back 0xffffffff from every transaction, which is consistent with
+	 * mdiobus_read() taking an uninitialised mutex). At this point in
+	 * probe() the bus is not yet visible to anything else, so there is no
+	 * concurrent access to guard against and no lock is needed yet.
+	 */
+	ret = bus->write(bus, QCA8084_PAGE_PHY, QCA8084_PAGE_REG,
+			  (addr & 0xffffff) >> 8);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	udelay(100);
+
+	lo = bus->read(bus, QCA8084_DATA_PHY(addr), addr & 0x1c);
+	if (lo < 0)
+		return lo;
+	hi = bus->read(bus, QCA8084_DATA_PHY(addr), (addr & 0x1c) + 2);
+	if (hi < 0)
+		return hi;
+
+	*val = ((u32)(u16)lo) | (((u32)(u16)hi) << 16);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int qca8084_reg_write32(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 addr, u32 val)
+{
+	int ret;
+	u32 slot = addr & 0x1c;
+
+	ret = bus->write(bus, QCA8084_PAGE_PHY, QCA8084_PAGE_REG,
+			  (addr & 0xffffff) >> 8);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	udelay(100);
+
+	ret = bus->write(bus, QCA8084_DATA_PHY(addr), slot, val & 0xffff);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	return bus->write(bus, QCA8084_DATA_PHY(addr), slot + 2, val >> 16);
+}
+
+/* Set bit 0 (transcribed from FUN_4a94c568; meaning inferred as
+ * "clock enable" from context, not confirmed against a datasheet).
+ */
+/* WARNING: the first part of this struct is an ABI shared with an out-of-tree
+ * module and must not be reordered.
+ *
+ * qca-ssdk reaches straight into bus->priv and casts it to its own
+ * struct qca_mdio_data (include/init/ssdk_plat.h), then calls sw_read and
+ * sw_write at fixed offsets to do 32-bit switch register access. There is no
+ * negotiation and no version check: if the layouts disagree it will call
+ * whatever pointer-shaped bytes happen to sit at those offsets.
+ *
+ * Until now they did disagree, and the only reason this did not crash is that
+ * the bytes landing on sw_read happened to be NULL, so qca_mii_raw_read()
+ * took its failure path and returned the 0xffffffff its caller had
+ * initialised. That is exactly why the ssdk reported "Unsupported clock mode
+ * 63": 63 is 0x3f, and 0x3f is 0xffffffff masked with MHT_WORK_MODE_MASK. It
+ * was never reading the work mode at all.
+ *
+ * The ssdk only ever touches clk_div, preinit, sw_read and sw_write, but the
+ * fields before them still have to be present so the offsets line up. Anything
+ * of ours goes after sw_write, where its own flexible clk[] member sits.
+ */
+#define IPQ4019_ETH_LDO_RDY_CNT		3
+
 struct ipq4019_mdio_data {
-	void __iomem	*membase;
-	void __iomem *eth_ldo_rdy;
+	/* --- shared layout, keep in step with struct qca_mdio_data --- */
+	void __iomem *membase[2];
+	void __iomem *eth_ldo_rdy[IPQ4019_ETH_LDO_RDY_CNT];
+	int clk_div;
+	bool force_c22;
+	struct gpio_descs *reset_gpios;
+	void (*preinit)(struct mii_bus *bus);
+	u32 (*sw_read)(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 reg);
+	void (*sw_write)(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 reg, u32 val);
+	/* --- ours --- */
 	struct clk *mdio_clk;
 	unsigned int mdc_rate;
 };
 
+/* Write, read back, and say in the log whether the value actually stuck.
+ *
+ * Every write in this sequence used to be issued blind, and that mattered:
+ * for three builds the package was held in reset and every one of them was
+ * a silent no-op that nothing reported. This runs once, at probe, so the
+ * extra reads cost nothing worth counting, and a write that does not land
+ * is precisely the failure mode this bring-up keeps hitting.
+ *
+ * mask says which bits the verdict is about. Several of these registers
+ * carry status bits alongside the control bit, so a whole-word compare
+ * would cry wolf; pass just the bits being set or cleared.
+ */
+static int qca8084_write_verify(struct mii_bus *bus, const char *what,
+				u32 reg, u32 val, u32 mask)
+{
+	u32 got;
+	int ret = qca8084_reg_write32(bus, reg, val);
+
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: %s 0x%08x write failed (%d)\n",
+			 what, reg, ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, reg, &got);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: %s 0x%08x read-back failed (%d)\n",
+			 what, reg, ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+		 "qca8084: %s 0x%08x <- 0x%08x, reads 0x%08x -- masked %08x %s\n",
+		 what, reg, val, got, mask,
+		 (got & mask) == (val & mask) ? "LANDED" : "DID NOT LAND");
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int qca8084_clk_enable(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 reg)
+{
+	u32 val;
+	int ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, reg, &val);
+
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	return qca8084_write_verify(bus, "clk enable", reg, val | 1, 1);
+}
+
+/* Assert bit 2, wait ~21ms, deassert (transcribed from FUN_4a94c57e;
+ * meaning inferred as "reset pulse" from the assert/wait/deassert shape).
+ */
+static int qca8084_reset_pulse(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 reg)
+{
+	u32 val;
+	int ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, reg, &val);
+
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	ret = qca8084_write_verify(bus, "reset assert", reg, val | 4, 4);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	usleep_range(21000, 22000);
+	return qca8084_write_verify(bus, "reset release", reg, val & ~4u, 4);
+}
+
+/* Standard Qualcomm PHY "debug register" access: write the sub-register
+ * address to MDIO reg 0x1d, then the value is read/written via reg 0x1e.
+ * The same convention mainline's at803x.c already uses for other Qualcomm
+ * PHYs, applied here to each EPHY's own MDIO address.
+ */
+static int qca8084_debug_write(struct mii_bus *bus, int phy_addr,
+				u16 debug_reg, u16 mask, u16 set)
+{
+	int val, ret;
+
+	ret = bus->write(bus, phy_addr, 0x1d, debug_reg);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	val = bus->read(bus, phy_addr, 0x1e);
+	if (val < 0)
+		return val;
+	ret = bus->write(bus, phy_addr, 0x1d, debug_reg);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	return bus->write(bus, phy_addr, 0x1e, (val & ~mask) | set);
+}
+
+/* Per-port calibration source registers and the bitfield extraction each
+ * one needs -- transcribed exactly from the decompiled calibration loop
+ * (FUN_4a94c630), not re-derived. Ports 1 and 2 share the same extraction
+ * shape and only differ in which register they read.
+ */
+struct qca8084_calib_src {
+	u32 reg;
+	u32 trim_a_shift, trim_a_mask;
+	u32 trim_b_shift, trim_b_mask;
+};
+
+static const struct qca8084_calib_src qca8084_calib_srcs[4] = {
+	/* port 0 */ { 0x0c900048, 0x12, 0x3fffff, 0x16, 0x7ffffff },
+	/* port 1 */ { 0x0c900060, 0x17, 0x7ffffff, 0x1b, 0xffffffff },
+	/* port 2 */ { 0x0c900068, 0x17, 0x7ffffff, 0x1b, 0xffffffff },
+	/* port 3 */ { 0x0c90005c, 0x0e, 0x3ffff,   0x12, 0x7fffff },
+};
+
+/* Program a root clock generator and commit it.
+ *
+ * Straight from the vendor's own Linux driver, qca-ssdk
+ * src/init/ssdk_mht_clk.c: the config register holding the source select
+ * lives at MHT_CLK_BASE_REG + clk->rcg, and the command register that
+ * commits it is four bytes BELOW that, at + clk->rcg - 4. Setting
+ * RCGR_CMD_UPDATE starts the update and the hardware clears it when done.
+ *
+ * This whole step was missing. The bring-up transcribed from the bootloader
+ * only ever set the enable bit in the branch registers (CBCR bit 0), never
+ * programmed or committed the RCG that feeds them, so the branches were
+ * being enabled with no committed source. That is consistent with the one
+ * write that has ever refused to land on this board -- MEM_CTRL, which is
+ * switch-core state and would not be writable with the core unclocked.
+ */
+static int qca8084_rcg_commit(struct mii_bus *bus, const char *what,
+			      u32 rcg_off, u32 src, u32 div)
+{
+	u32 cfg_reg = QCA8084_CLK_BASE + rcg_off;
+	u32 cmd_reg = QCA8084_CLK_BASE + rcg_off - 4;
+	u32 val;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, cfg_reg, &val);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: %s RCG cfg read failed (%d)\n",
+			 what, ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	dev_info(bus->parent,
+		 "qca8084: %s RCG cfg 0x%08x before = 0x%08x (src %u, div %u)\n",
+		 what, cfg_reg, val, (val & QCA8084_RCGR_SRC_SEL) >> 8,
+		 val & QCA8084_RCGR_HDIV);
+
+	val &= ~QCA8084_RCGR_SRC_SEL;
+	val |= (src << 8) & QCA8084_RCGR_SRC_SEL;
+	val &= ~QCA8084_RCGR_HDIV;
+	val |= div & QCA8084_RCGR_HDIV;
+	ret = qca8084_reg_write32(bus, cfg_reg, val);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, cmd_reg, &val);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	ret = qca8084_reg_write32(bus, cmd_reg, val | QCA8084_RCGR_CMD_UPDATE);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
+		if (qca8084_reg_read32(bus, cmd_reg, &val))
+			break;
+		if (!(val & QCA8084_RCGR_CMD_UPDATE)) {
+			dev_info(bus->parent,
+				 "qca8084: %s RCG committed, src %u div %u, after %d us\n",
+				 what, src, div, i);
+			return 0;
+		}
+		udelay(1);
+	}
+
+	dev_warn(bus->parent,
+		 "qca8084: %s RCG update never cleared (cmd 0x%08x = 0x%08x)\n",
+		 what, cmd_reg, val);
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
+/* The 32-bit switch register accessors qca-ssdk calls through bus->priv.
+ *
+ * The register it hands over carries a 4-bit type tag in bits [31:28]
+ * (SSDK_SWITCH_REG_TYPE_MASK). For the QCA8386 that tag is zero, so masking it
+ * off is a no-op today, but the ssdk does set it unconditionally and a QCA8337
+ * would arrive tagged 1, so strip it rather than assume.
+ *
+ * No locking here. The ssdk's qca_mii_read()/qca_mii_write() already take
+ * bus->mdio_lock around the call, and its __qca_mii_read() variant
+ * deliberately does not; taking it here would deadlock the first and change
+ * the meaning of the second. These reach bus->read/bus->write directly for the
+ * same reason the preinit path does.
+ */
+static u32 qca8084_sw_read(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 reg)
+{
+	u32 val = 0xffffffff;
+
+	qca8084_reg_read32(bus, reg & ~SSDK_SWITCH_REG_TYPE_MASK, &val);
+	return val;
+}
+
+static void qca8084_sw_write(struct mii_bus *bus, u32 reg, u32 val)
+{
+	qca8084_reg_write32(bus, reg & ~SSDK_SWITCH_REG_TYPE_MASK, val);
+}
+
+/* Sample and log each EPHY's link state.
+ *
+ * BMSR bit 2 is link status and it latches low, so read it twice and trust the
+ * second. Register 0x11 is the QCA808x-family PHY-specific status: bit 10 is
+ * link, bits [15:14] speed (0 = 10M, 1 = 100M, 2 = 1000M, 3 = 2500M), bit 13
+ * duplex.
+ *
+ * Timing is the whole point of taking this more than once. Autonegotiation
+ * takes seconds, and preinit runs about two seconds into boot, so a single
+ * sample taken right after the PHY resets will always say "link down" whether
+ * or not a cable is present -- which is exactly the misleading result that
+ * prompted this.
+ */
+static void qca8084_sample_ephy_link(struct mii_bus *bus, const char *when)
+{
+	int addr;
+
+	for (addr = 1; addr <= 4; addr++) {
+		int bmcr, bmsr, stat;
+
+		bmcr = bus->read(bus, addr, MII_BMCR);
+		bus->read(bus, addr, MII_BMSR);		/* clear the latch */
+		bmsr = bus->read(bus, addr, MII_BMSR);
+		stat = bus->read(bus, addr, 0x11);
+
+		if (bmcr < 0 || bmsr < 0)
+			continue;
+
+		dev_info(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: EPHY %d %s: BMCR 0x%04x BMSR 0x%04x link %s | STATUS 0x%04x link %s speed %s %s\n",
+			 addr, when, bmcr, bmsr,
+			 (bmsr & BMSR_LSTATUS) ? "UP" : "down",
+			 stat,
+			 (stat & BIT(10)) ? "UP" : "down",
+			 ((stat >> 14) & 3) == 3 ? "2500M" :
+			 ((stat >> 14) & 3) == 2 ? "1000M" :
+			 ((stat >> 14) & 3) == 1 ? "100M" : "10M",
+			 (stat & BIT(13)) ? "full" : "half");
+	}
+}
+
+static void qca8084_preinit(struct mii_bus *bus)
+{
+	static const u32 clk_a = 0x0c8001a8, clk_b = 0x0c8001ac;
+	static const u32 ephy_clk[4] = { 0x0c8001b0, 0x0c8001b4,
+					  0x0c8001b8, 0x0c8001bc };
+	struct gpio_desc *reset_gpio;
+	u32 ephy_cfg, gate_reg, reg_val;
+	bool vendor_gate = false;
+	u32 delay_us, post_delay_us;
+	u32 reg;
+	int port, ret;
+
+	/* Is the SoC's CMN PLL actually running?
+	 *
+	 * This is a diagnostic for the leading explanation of why the four
+	 * EPHYs never appear. The CMN PLL at 0x0009b000 generates the ethernet
+	 * reference clocks handed to the external PHY package. If it is not
+	 * locked, the QCA8084's own gp0..gp3_sys_clk domains have nothing to
+	 * run from, so the four EPHY cores and their MDIO slaves are dead --
+	 * while the switch die's register window, clocked by this host's own
+	 * MDC, keeps answering and keeps latching every write we read back.
+	 * That is exactly the split we observe, and it is the one class of
+	 * explanation in which "all 27 writes verified" proves nothing, since
+	 * none of those registers are inside the EPHY clock domain.
+	 *
+	 * Bit 2 of 0x0009b064 is the lock status. Read it directly rather than
+	 * inferring anything: if it is already set then the bootloader locked
+	 * the PLL, and this whole line of reasoning is refuted for free.
+	 */
+	{
+		void __iomem *cmn = ioremap(0x0009b000, 0x800);
+
+		if (!cmn) {
+			dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: cmn pll ioremap failed\n");
+		} else {
+			u32 lock = readl(cmn + 0x064);
+
+			dev_info(bus->parent,
+				 "qca8084: CMN PLL 9b064=%08x (lock bit2 %s) 9b028=%08x 9b780=%08x 9b784=%08x\n",
+				 lock, (lock & 0x4) ? "SET, locked" : "CLEAR, NOT locked",
+				 readl(cmn + 0x028), readl(cmn + 0x780),
+				 readl(cmn + 0x784));
+			iounmap(cmn);
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* Sampled before this driver touches anything. On a cold boot the chip
+	 * is still in reset and this will read nothing useful, but after an
+	 * unbind/rebind it reports the state the previous session settled into,
+	 * cable and autonegotiation included. That is the only runtime view of
+	 * PHY link this port has:
+	 *
+	 *   echo 90000.mdio > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/ipq4019-mdio/unbind
+	 *   echo 90000.mdio > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/ipq4019-mdio/bind
+	 */
+	qca8084_sample_ephy_link(bus, "as found");
+
+	/* The bus-level PHY reset line.
+	 *
+	 * This is the second consequence of moving preinit before
+	 * of_mdiobus_register(), and it is why the previous attempt at the new
+	 * ordering still read 0xffffffff even after the mdio_lock fix.
+	 * __mdiobus_register() is what claims the bus's reset GPIO and pulses
+	 * it (devm_gpiod_get_optional(&bus->dev, "reset", GPIOD_OUT_HIGH),
+	 * then assert / reset-delay-us / deassert / reset-post-delay-us).
+	 * Running before registration means that has not happened yet: on this
+	 * board tlmm gpio24 is still unclaimed with a pull-down, i.e. sitting
+	 * low, and the line is GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW, so the QCA8386 package is held
+	 * in hardware reset for the whole of preinit. A chip in reset answers
+	 * every MDIO transaction with all-1s.
+	 *
+	 * Consistent with both things already observed on this hardware: reads
+	 * worked and returned 0x00318820 when preinit ran after registration
+	 * (line deasserted, physically high), and return all-1s when it runs
+	 * before (line never claimed, physically low).
+	 *
+	 * So preinit takes ownership of the line. The DTS deliberately names
+	 * the property "qca8084-reset-gpios" rather than "reset-gpios": if it
+	 * were still called "reset-gpios" then (a) __mdiobus_register() would
+	 * re-request it and fail with -EBUSY, and (b) even if it did not, it
+	 * would re-pulse the line after preinit and throw away the clock,
+	 * reset and calibration work done here.
+	 */
+	reset_gpio = devm_gpiod_get_optional(bus->parent, "qca8084-reset",
+					     GPIOD_ASIS);
+	if (IS_ERR(reset_gpio)) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: reset gpio get failed (%ld)\n",
+			 PTR_ERR(reset_gpio));
+		reset_gpio = NULL;
+	}
+
+	if (reset_gpio) {
+		/* Logged before we drive anything: this is the diagnostic that
+		 * decides whether the reset line really was the blocker. A
+		 * logical 1 here means asserted, i.e. the package was in reset
+		 * on entry, which is the predicted state.
+		 */
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: reset gpio as found = %d (1 = asserted, in reset)\n",
+			 gpiod_get_value_cansleep(reset_gpio));
+
+		if (of_property_read_u32(bus->parent->of_node,
+					 "reset-delay-us", &delay_us))
+			delay_us = 10000;
+		if (of_property_read_u32(bus->parent->of_node,
+					 "reset-post-delay-us", &post_delay_us))
+			post_delay_us = 50000;
+
+		gpiod_direction_output(reset_gpio, 1);
+		fsleep(delay_us);
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(reset_gpio, 0);
+		fsleep(post_delay_us);
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: reset pulsed and deasserted (%u us / %u us)\n",
+			 delay_us, post_delay_us);
+	} else {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: no qca8084-reset-gpios in DTS, package may still be held in reset\n");
+	}
+
+	/* The MDIO controller's own reset/clock-divider setup also normally
+	 * runs as part of bus registration, so do it here too. bus->reset is
+	 * already assigned by this point in probe() and only touches priv
+	 * fields (membase, mdio_clk) set up earlier in probe(), so it is safe
+	 * to call directly without the bus being registered.
+	 */
+	if (bus->reset)
+		bus->reset(bus);
+
+	/* Pulse the chip's own global reset before configuring anything.
+	 *
+	 * 0x0c800308 bit 0 is GCC_GLOBAL_CTL_GCC_TOP_FUNC_ARES in the vendor's
+	 * register map, and the same bit is MHT_GLOBAL_RST in qca-ssdk's clock
+	 * lookup table. It is the only documented reset in this chip that this
+	 * driver has never used: the package reset GPIO resets from outside,
+	 * and the per-branch CBCR bit 2 resets one clock domain, but this is
+	 * the internal reset of the whole top-level function block.
+	 *
+	 * Done here, before any configuration, so that the work mode, memory
+	 * config, MDIO addresses and clock setup that follow all land on a
+	 * chip in a known state rather than whatever the straps plus a partial
+	 * bootloader left behind.
+	 */
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_GLOBAL_CTL, &reg_val);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: GLOBAL_CTL read failed (%d)\n", ret);
+	} else {
+		dev_info(bus->parent, "qca8084: GLOBAL_CTL before = 0x%08x\n",
+			 reg_val);
+		qca8084_reg_write32(bus, QCA8084_GLOBAL_CTL, reg_val | 1u);
+		usleep_range(10000, 11000);
+		qca8084_reg_write32(bus, QCA8084_GLOBAL_CTL, reg_val & ~1u);
+		usleep_range(10000, 11000);
+		if (!qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_GLOBAL_CTL, &reg_val))
+			dev_info(bus->parent,
+				 "qca8084: GLOBAL_CTL after pulse = 0x%08x\n",
+				 reg_val);
+	}
+
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG, &ephy_cfg);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: EPHY_CFG read failed (%d), aborting preinit\n",
+			 ret);
+		return;
+	}
+	qca8084_dbg(bus->parent, "qca8084: EPHY_CFG = 0x%08x before preinit\n",
+		 ephy_cfg);
+	for (port = 0; port < 4; port++)
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent, "qca8084: port %d strap addr = 0x%x\n",
+			 port, (ephy_cfg >> (port * 5)) & 0x1f);
+
+	/* Diagnostic, and the thing that makes this boot conclusive either
+	 * way. If EPHY_CFG still reads all-1s then the reset line was not the
+	 * (only) blocker, and the next question is whether ANY device answers
+	 * Clause 22 on this bus at this point in probe -- which distinguishes
+	 * "the chip is not responding" from "the MDIO controller itself is not
+	 * usable yet here". Reading PHY ID register 2 from all 32 addresses
+	 * answers that in one boot instead of another build cycle.
+	 */
+	if (ephy_cfg == 0xffffffff) {
+		int addr, found = 0;
+
+		dev_warn(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: EPHY_CFG still all-1s, scanning bus for any C22 responder\n");
+		for (addr = 0; addr < 32; addr++) {
+			int id2 = bus->read(bus, addr, MII_PHYSID2);
+
+			if (id2 >= 0 && id2 != 0xffff) {
+				dev_warn(bus->parent,
+					 "qca8084: scan: addr %d answers, PHYSID2 = 0x%04x\n",
+					 addr, id2);
+				found++;
+			}
+		}
+		if (!found)
+			dev_warn(bus->parent,
+				 "qca8084: scan: no device answered on any of the 32 addresses\n");
+	}
+
+	/* Bring up the clocks the branch enables below actually depend on.
+	 *
+	 * The switch core clock is enabled here and was not enabled anywhere
+	 * before. In switch mode the core has to be running for its own state
+	 * -- including the memory configuration written just above -- to be
+	 * writable at all.
+	 *
+	 * Parent select 0 is the 50 MHz XO for both, per mht_switch_core_pdata
+	 * and mht_sys_clk_pdata in the vendor driver, whose first entry is
+	 * { MHT_XO_CLK_RATE_50M, MHT_P_XO, 0 }.
+	 */
+	/* The switch core RCG is deliberately left alone.
+	 *
+	 * It wants 312.5 MHz, and per mht_switch_core_pdata the only parent that
+	 * can produce that is index 1, UNIPHY1_TX312P5M. But that clock comes
+	 * from the SoC's uniphy, which is not up this early, so committing the
+	 * switch to it here fails: the update bit never clears.
+	 *
+	 *   switch core RCG update never cleared (cmd 0x0c800000 = 0x00000001)
+	 *
+	 * qca-ssdk repoints this parent itself once the uniphy is running, in
+	 * ssdk_mht_gcc_common_clk_parent_enable(). Enabling the branch is still
+	 * right and is kept.
+	 */
+	qca8084_clk_enable(bus, QCA8084_SWITCH_CORE_CBCR);
+	qca8084_reset_pulse(bus, QCA8084_SWITCH_CORE_CBCR);
+
+	/* The four GEPHY sys clocks and the two SerDes sys clocks all hang off
+	 * this one RCG (0x1a4 in every one of their CLK_LOOKUP entries), so it
+	 * has to be committed once before their branches are enabled further
+	 * down.
+	 */
+	/* The four GEPHY and two SerDes sys clocks want 25 MHz from the 50 MHz
+	 * XO (mht_sys_clk_pdata, mht_sys_clk_support_rates), so src 0 and
+	 * div = (50 * 2 / 25) - 1 = 3. Only the source was being set before,
+	 * leaving the divider at whatever reset left behind -- and a GEPHY
+	 * running its DSP off the wrong clock still answers MDIO perfectly
+	 * while never achieving link, which is exactly what was observed.
+	 */
+	qca8084_rcg_commit(bus, "gephy/serdes sys", 0x1a4, 0, 3);
+
+	/* Read the SKU the way the vendor reads it: with the MDIO clock slowed
+	 * right down, then put back.
+	 *
+	 * qca_mht_sku_check() calls ssdk_miibus_freq_set(..., 0xff) before
+	 * touching a fuse row and restores the old frequency straight after,
+	 * with the comment "fuse register need use lower mdio clock to read".
+	 * Reading it at the normal rate gave 0x00000265, which matches none of
+	 * the four known SKUs, and that is worth exactly nothing until this
+	 * read is done properly. If it still says 0x265 at the slowest clock,
+	 * then the assumption that this part is a QCA8386 is what needs
+	 * re-examining, and that would reframe everything.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct ipq4019_mdio_data *priv = bus->priv;
+		u32 saved_mode, slow_mode, row0 = 0, row2 = 0;
+
+		saved_mode = readl(priv->membase[0] + MDIO_MODE_REG);
+		slow_mode = (saved_mode & ~MDIO_MODE_DIV_MASK) | MDIO_MODE_DIV(256);
+		writel(slow_mode, priv->membase[0] + MDIO_MODE_REG);
+		/* let the slower clock settle before trusting a fuse read */
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+
+		qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_QFPROM_PTE_ROW0, &row0);
+		qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_QFPROM_PTE_ROW2, &row2);
+
+		writel(saved_mode, priv->membase[0] + MDIO_MODE_REG);
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+
+		dev_info(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: SKU fuse at slow mdc (mode 0x%08x -> 0x%08x): row0 = 0x%08x, sku field 0x%05x (8082=0x1dc 8084=0x1dd 8085=0x1de 8386=0x1df), row2 = 0x%08x\n",
+			 saved_mode, slow_mode, row0, row0 & 0xfffff, row2);
+	}
+
+	/* and again at the normal rate, so the two are directly comparable */
+	if (!qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_QFPROM_PTE_ROW0, &reg_val))
+		dev_info(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: SKU fuse at normal mdc: 0x%08x, sku field 0x%05x\n",
+			 reg_val, reg_val & 0xfffff);
+
+	if (!qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_MDIO_CTRL0, &reg_val))
+		dev_info(bus->parent, "qca8084: MDIO_CTRL0 = 0x%08x\n", reg_val);
+	if (!qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_MDIO_CTRL1, &reg_val))
+		dev_info(bus->parent, "qca8084: MDIO_CTRL1 = 0x%08x\n", reg_val);
+
+	/* Assign the MDIO addresses, BEFORE the bring-up sequence below.
+	 *
+	 * This is a separate vendor function, FUN_4a94c7d0, missing entirely
+	 * from the first transcription, and the order matters. FUN_4a94b918
+	 * calls it at 0x4a94baa6 and then calls the bring-up routine at
+	 * 0x4a94baaa -- two consecutive bl instructions, address assignment
+	 * first. Running it after the bring-up instead left the four EPHYs
+	 * silent on the bus even though the writes provably landed (the two
+	 * companion devices moved from addresses 4,5 to 5,6 exactly as asked).
+	 * That fits the addresses being latched when the bring-up routine
+	 * clears EPHY_CFG bits [21:20] as its final step: set them after that
+	 * and there is nothing left to latch them.
+	 *
+	 * The four EPHYs do not stay where they are strapped. EPHY_CFG reads
+	 * 0x00318820 out of reset, i.e. strap addresses 0,1,2,3, but the
+	 * vendor overwrites the low 20 bits with a hardcoded 0x20c41, which
+	 * is the same four 5-bit fields holding 1,2,3,4:
+	 *
+	 *     0x20c41 = 1 | (2 << 5) | (3 << 10) | (4 << 15)
+	 *
+	 * So the vendor DTS's phy_address = <1>,<2>,<3>,<4> was right all
+	 * along, and reading the strap and "correcting" the DTS to 0,1,2,3
+	 * was reading the power-on default rather than the address the chip
+	 * is actually driven to. The DTS is set back to 1,2,3,4 to match.
+	 *
+	 * The companion register QCA8084_MDIO_ADDR_CFG gets the next three
+	 * addresses. The vendor computes them with a first-free-bit scan over
+	 * a hardcoded in-use bitmap of 0xffff001e -- bits 1-4 for the EPHYs
+	 * just placed, and bits 16-31 for the switch's own register window.
+	 * That bitmap is a constant, so the scan is deterministic and yields
+	 * 5, 6, 7; the result is written directly rather than porting the
+	 * scan. 0x1cc5 = 5 | (6 << 5) | (7 << 10).
+	 *
+	 * The 16-31 half of that bitmap independently matches what the bus
+	 * scan sees on this board: every address from 0x10 to 0x1f answers,
+	 * all reporting PHY ID 0x00010001, which is the switch window
+	 * aliasing rather than sixteen real PHYs.
+	 */
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG, &ephy_cfg);
+	if (!ret) {
+		qca8084_reg_write32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG,
+				    (ephy_cfg & 0xfff00000u) | 0x20c41u);
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: EPHY MDIO addresses assigned 1,2,3,4\n");
+	}
+
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_MDIO_ADDR_CFG, &reg_val);
+	if (!ret) {
+		qca8084_reg_write32(bus, QCA8084_MDIO_ADDR_CFG,
+				    (reg_val & ~0x7fffu) | 0x1cc5u);
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: companion MDIO addresses assigned 5,6,7\n");
+	}
+	usleep_range(11000, 12000);
+
+	/* clock enable, then reset-pulse, both shared blocks first */
+	qca8084_clk_enable(bus, clk_a);
+	qca8084_clk_enable(bus, clk_b);
+	qca8084_reset_pulse(bus, clk_a);
+	qca8084_reset_pulse(bus, clk_b);
+
+	/* clear bit 0 across 8 registers 0x058..0x138 step 0x20 -- transcribed
+	 * as-is from FUN_4a94c630; meaning not confirmed (isolate/bypass?).
+	 */
+	for (reg = 0x0c800058; reg != 0x0c800158; reg += 0x20) {
+		u32 val;
+
+		ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, reg, &val);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_warn(bus->parent,
+				 "qca8084: isolate-clear read 0x%x failed (%d)\n",
+				 reg, ret);
+			continue;
+		}
+		qca8084_write_verify(bus, "isolate-clear", reg, val & ~1u, 1);
+	}
+
+	/* per-EPHY clock enable, then reset-pulse, all 4 shared blocks first */
+	for (port = 0; port < 4; port++)
+		qca8084_clk_enable(bus, ephy_clk[port]);
+	for (port = 0; port < 4; port++)
+		qca8084_reset_pulse(bus, ephy_clk[port]);
+
+	/* Clear bits [4:0] of QCA8084_EPHY_HOLD. Transcribed from
+	 * FUN_4a94c630, where it sits exactly here, between the per-EPHY
+	 * reset pulses and the calibration gate read. It was missed in the
+	 * first transcription of the routine; Ghidra's literal pool resolves
+	 * the operand to 0x0c800304.
+	 *
+	 * Named "hold" on the guess that these five bits are a per-EPHY
+	 * hold/isolate, one per EPHY plus one shared -- the meaning is not
+	 * confirmed against a datasheet, only its position in the sequence.
+	 */
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_HOLD, &reg_val);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: EPHY_HOLD read failed (%d)\n", ret);
+	} else {
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent, "qca8084: EPHY_HOLD before = 0x%08x\n",
+			 reg_val);
+		qca8084_write_verify(bus, "EPHY_HOLD", QCA8084_EPHY_HOLD,
+				     reg_val & 0xffffffe0u, 0x1f);
+	}
+
+	/* Calibration loop is gated: only runs if this status field is 1 or 2.
+	 * Meaning not confirmed (chip variant / package mode?), transcribed
+	 * as-is.
+	 */
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_CALIB_GATE, &gate_reg);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: gate register read failed (%d)\n",
+			 ret);
+	} else {
+		vendor_gate = (((gate_reg & 0xffffff) >> 0x10) - 1) < 2;
+
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: gate register 0x%08x, vendor calibration condition %s\n",
+			 gate_reg, vendor_gate ? "true" : "false");
+	}
+	/* The gate is honoured again rather than bypassed. Bypassing it was a
+	 * diagnostic from when this register could only be read as 0xffffffff;
+	 * now that the package is out of reset it reads a real 0x00080000, and
+	 * the vendor's own condition on that value is false:
+	 *
+	 *     (((0x00080000 & 0xffffff) >> 16) - 1) = 7, which is not < 2
+	 *
+	 * So the stock firmware, which does bring Ethernet up on this board,
+	 * skips the calibration loop on this silicon. Running it anyway is the
+	 * unvalidated deviation, not the other way round.
+	 */
+	/* The gate is bypassed again, and this time on evidence rather than as a
+	 * shot in the dark.
+	 *
+	 * The gate is a field in a fuse row, and the vendor runs the calibration
+	 * only when it reads 1 or 2. On this part it reads 8. But this part also
+	 * reports an SKU of 0x265, which is none of the four the vendor driver
+	 * knows (QCA8082 0x1dc, QCA8084 0x1dd, QCA8085 0x1de, QCA8386 0x1df),
+	 * confirmed at the slowest MDIO clock as well as the normal one. The
+	 * vendor's interpretation of its own fuse fields cannot be assumed to
+	 * carry over to a variant it does not enumerate.
+	 *
+	 * What this calibration does is write fuse-derived trim values into each
+	 * EPHY's analogue debug registers. Skipping it leaves the analogue front
+	 * end untrimmed, and the symptom fits exactly: all four PHYs answer MDIO
+	 * perfectly, advertise sensible capabilities in BMSR, and never complete
+	 * autonegotiation or raise link with a live cable in.
+	 *
+	 * It was run once before with the gate bypassed and made no difference,
+	 * but that was while the indirect protocol was still dropping address
+	 * bits [7:5], so the trim sources were read from the wrong registers and
+	 * the values written were meaningless. It is worth exactly one honest
+	 * attempt now that the addressing is correct.
+	 */
+	if (!ret) {
+		for (port = 0; port < 4; port++) {
+			const struct qca8084_calib_src *src = &qca8084_calib_srcs[port];
+			u32 srcval, ephy_cfg_now;
+			u32 trim_a, trim_b;
+			int phy_addr;
+
+			ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG, &ephy_cfg_now);
+			if (ret)
+				break;
+			phy_addr = (ephy_cfg_now >> (port * 5)) & 0x1f;
+
+			ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, src->reg, &srcval);
+			if (ret)
+				break;
+			trim_a = (srcval & src->trim_a_mask) >> src->trim_a_shift;
+			/* AND with 0xffffffff is a no-op; ports 1/2 use that as the
+			 * mask because the decompiled source applies no mask there
+			 * at all before the shift -- kept explicit rather than
+			 * special-cased, so this line means the same thing for
+			 * every port.
+			 */
+			trim_b = (srcval & src->trim_b_mask) >> src->trim_b_shift;
+
+			qca8084_debug_write(bus, phy_addr, 0x180, 0x00f0,
+					     (trim_a & 0xf) << 4);
+			qca8084_debug_write(bus, phy_addr, 0x280, 0x001f,
+					     trim_b & 0x1f);
+
+			qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+				 "qca8084: port %d (phy 0x%x) calibrated, trim_a=0x%x trim_b=0x%x\n",
+				 port, phy_addr, trim_a, trim_b);
+		}
+	} else if (!ret) {
+		qca8084_dbg(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: gate condition false, calibration loop skipped (matches the vendor)\n");
+	}
+
+	/* final step of FUN_4a94c630: clear bits [21:20] of EPHY_CFG, settle */
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG, &ephy_cfg);
+	if (!ret) {
+		qca8084_reg_write32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG, ephy_cfg & 0xffcfffffu);
+		usleep_range(11000, 12000);
+	}
+
+	ret = qca8084_reg_read32(bus, QCA8084_EPHY_CFG, &ephy_cfg);
+	if (!ret)
+		dev_info(bus->parent,
+			 "qca8084: preinit done, EPHY_CFG = 0x%08x\n", ephy_cfg);
+	else
+		dev_warn(bus->parent, "qca8084: preinit done, EPHY_CFG unreadable\n");
+
+	/* The probes that lived here -- a write-mask sweep of MEM_CTRL and
+	 * friends, a dump of the fuse block, and a sweep of every work mode --
+	 * have done their job and are removed. Between them they exposed the
+	 * dropped address bits in the indirect protocol, which was the actual
+	 * bug. They were also destructive: the write-mask probe deliberately
+	 * writes all-ones to registers it does not understand, and the sweep
+	 * left the chip in whichever mode it tried last, thirteen seconds
+	 * before qca-ssdk probes. Neither is a thing to leave running.
+	 *
+	 * Recorded so they are not re-derived:
+	 *   WORK_MODE strap really reads 0x3f, not the 0x0f the aliased read
+	 *   reported, and the EPHYs answer in all five defined work modes, so
+	 *   work mode is not what gates them.
+	 *   MEM_CTRL reads 0x00000021 with writable mask 0x00000030, which is
+	 *   exactly the two DVS bits, so the vendor's memory configuration
+	 *   step is performable after all.
+	 */
+
+	/* Put the package into switch mode -- LAST, after every reset.
+	 *
+	 * This was originally done early, before the clocks. That was wrong,
+	 * and the symptom was subtle: the write landed and read back 0x10, but
+	 * by the time qca-ssdk probed thirteen seconds later the register had
+	 * returned to its strap value and the driver bailed out with
+	 * "ssdk_mht_gcc_port_clk_parent_set: Unsupported clock mode 63" -- 63
+	 * being 0x3f, the strap. The switch core reset pulse that followed the
+	 * write was reverting it. Anything that resets the core has to happen
+	 * before the mode is chosen, not after.
+	 *
+	 * This register has never been written by this driver, and it is the
+	 * one the vendor U-Boot's ipq_qca8084_work_mode_init writes. The name
+	 * came out of the firmware's own symbol strings; the register number
+	 * and the value came from something much better than decompilation,
+	 * namely the vendor's Linux driver, which is sitting in this build
+	 * tree: qca-ssdk, include/hsl/mht/mht_reg.h and
+	 * src/hsl/mht/mht_sec_ctrl.c.
